hypotheses.statement:
Fix directions $v_1,\ldots,v_T$ satisfying $$\label{eq:frames:bases}
 (v_s,\ldots,v_{s+d-1})\text{ is a basis of }V
 \qquad(1\le s\le T-d+1).$$

$$\label{eq:marginal-constants}
 k=64,\qquad m=\frac{n}{k},\qquad
 \alpha=\frac1{4k},\qquad T=(1+16k)d=1025d,$$ and assume $d\ge6$. Put $$\label{eq:marginal-error}
 b_n=2\left(\frac{2}{3^{3/4}}\right)^m,\qquad
 \varepsilon_n=\frac{n^{3/2}}2\sqrt{e^{-4d}+b_n}.$$ For a labeled partition $\mathcal M=(M_1,\ldots,M_k)$ of $V$ into $m$-element blocks, let $\delta_a(\mathcal M)$ be the distance of the time-$T$ images of $V\setminus M_a$ under the physical shuffle from the uniform distinct tuple law. Any fixed ordering of these labels gives the same distance.

Let a finite set $X$ be partitioned into $k\ge1$ nonempty sets $M_1,\ldots,M_k$, and put $G=\mathop{\mathrm{Sym}}(X)$. Let $H_a$ be the subgroup permuting $M_a$ and fixing $X\setminus M_a$ pointwise. Suppose that a probability measure $\nu$ on $G$ satisfies, for some $B\ge 1$, $$\label{eq:lift:coset-cap}
  \nu(gH_a)\le B\frac{|H_a|}{|G|}
  \qquad(g\in G,\ 1\le a\le k).$$ Then every irreducible complex unitary representation $\rho$ of $G$, of dimension $D$, satisfies $$\label{eq:lift:orbit-sum}
  \|\widehat\nu(\rho)\|_{\mathrm{op}}^2
  \le \frac{B}{D}\sum_{a=1}^k
       \sum_{\substack{\pi\in\widehat H_a\\\dim\pi\le D^{1/k}}}
                    (\dim\pi)^2.$$ The block sizes need not be equal.

outputs.exact_statement:
For every integer $t\ge0$, $$\label{eq:exact-support-lower}
 \|q_d^{*t}-U\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\ge 1-\frac{2^{tn/2}}{n!}.$$ Consequently, $$\label{eq:sharp-support-time}
 t_{\mathrm{mix}}(d)\ge
 \left\lceil\frac2n\log_2\left(\frac{3n!}{4}\right)\right\rceil
 =2d-O(1).$$ For $0\le t\le d$, $$\|q_d^{*t}-U\|_{\mathrm{TV}}\ge1-(e/\sqrt n)^n.$$ In particular, the distance tends to one uniformly for integer $t\le d$, and $t_{\mathrm{mix}}(d)\ge d$ for all sufficiently large $d$.

Under Lemma <a href="#lem:frames" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:frames">[lem:frames]</a>, let $0\le q\le n$ and let $\pi_q$ be uniform on ordered distinct $q$-tuples of positions. For every fixed ordered list $C$ of $q$ distinct labels, $$\label{eq:frames:tuple-input-map}
 \|\mathcal L(Z_T(C))-\pi_q\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
 =
 \|\mathcal L(X^{\boldsymbol\iota}_T(L_0^{-1}C))-\pi_q\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.$$ Thus the same-list distances agree when $L_0=I$. Their averages also agree when $C$ is sampled uniformly over ordered distinct lists, independently of the switch process: $$\label{eq:frames:averaged-input-map}
 \mathbb E_C\|\mathcal L(Z_T(C)\mid C)-\pi_q\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
 =
 \mathbb E_C\|\mathcal L(X^{\boldsymbol\iota}_T(C)\mid C)-\pi_q\|_{\mathrm{TV}}.$$

In the preceding setup let $\Lambda$ be terminal data that determine the complete direction schedule, and suppose $\Xi$ is independent of $(\Lambda,(Z_t)_{t=0}^T)$. For every $1\le j\le q$ and every $x\in V$, assume that its online terminal vector is also the conditional hidden law after these data and the complete predecessor paths are known: $$\label{eq:marginal-terminal-identification}
 \Pr\{Z_T(c_j)=x\mid
       \Lambda,\Xi,\,
       Z_s(c_i):0\le s\le T,\ i<j\}
 =p_T^j(x)\quad\text{almost surely}.$$ Then $$\label{shear:eq:tuple-bound}
 \mathbb E_{\Lambda,\Xi}
 \|\mathcal L(Z_T(C)\mid\Lambda,\Xi)-\pi_q\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
 \le\frac12\sum_{j=1}^q
       \sqrt{(n-j+1)\mathbb E\Delta_T^j}.$$ All expectations are under the actual joint law of the data, list, and process. If $\mathbb E\Delta_T^j\le a_T$ for every $j$, the right side is at most $q\sqrt n\,\sqrt{a_T}/2$. For $q=0$ both sides are zero.

There is a deterministic labeled partition $\mathcal M=(M_1,\ldots,M_k)$ into blocks of size $m$ for which $$\label{eq:marginal-partition}
 \sum_{a=1}^k\delta_a(\mathcal M)
 \le k\varepsilon_n=o(1)\qquad(d\longrightarrow\infty).$$

Let a finite set $X$ be partitioned into $k\ge1$ nonempty sets $M_1,\ldots,M_k$, and put $G=\mathop{\mathrm{Sym}}(X)$. Let $H_a$ be the subgroup permuting $M_a$ and fixing $X\setminus M_a$ pointwise. Suppose that a probability measure $\nu$ on $G$ satisfies, for some $B\ge 1$, $$\label{eq:lift:coset-cap}
  \nu(gH_a)\le B\frac{|H_a|}{|G|}
  \qquad(g\in G,\ 1\le a\le k).$$ Then every irreducible complex unitary representation $\rho$ of $G$, of dimension $D$, satisfies $$\label{eq:lift:orbit-sum}
  \|\widehat\nu(\rho)\|_{\mathrm{op}}^2
  \le \frac{B}{D}\sum_{a=1}^k
       \sum_{\substack{\pi\in\widehat H_a\\\dim\pi\le D^{1/k}}}
                    (\dim\pi)^2.$$ The block sizes need not be equal.

Let a finite set $X$ be partitioned into $k>18$ nonempty sets $M_1,\ldots,M_k$, and put $G=\mathop{\mathrm{Sym}}(X)$. Let $H_a$ be the subgroup that permutes $M_a$ and fixes $X\setminus M_a$ pointwise. Suppose that a probability measure $\nu$ on $G$ satisfies, for some $B\ge1$, $$\nu(gH_a)\le B\frac{|H_a|}{|G|}
 \qquad(g\in G,\ 1\le a\le k).$$ Then every irreducible complex unitary representation $\rho$ of $G$, of dimension $D$, satisfies $$\label{eq:lift:general-fourier}
 \|\widehat\nu(\rho)\|_{\mathrm{op}}
 \le\sqrt{kBC_0}\,D^{-(1-18/k)/2}.$$ Here $C_0$ is the absolute constant of Lemma <a href="#lem:degrees" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:degrees">12</a>. The block sizes need not be equal.

Fix any $\ell<N$ observed labels, and put $m=N-\ell$. For $s>d$, let $D_{s-1}$ be their available positions just before step $s$. Then $$\label{shear:eq:fixed-balance}
 \Pr\{\min(|D_{s-1}\cap W_s|,|D_{s-1}\setminus W_s|)<m/4\}
 \le2e^{-m/8}.$$ The probability averages the observed paths; it is not conditional on them.

Fix an integer $b\ge2$ dividing $N$, put $m=N/b$, and let $t\ge d$ be an integer. Let $C$ be a uniformly sampled ordered tuple of $N-m$ distinct input labels, independent of the shuffle, and let $\mu_C$ be its time-$t$ physical image law with $C$ fixed. Then $$\label{lifts:averaged-bound}
 \mathbb E_C\|\mu_C-\pi_{N-m}\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
 \le\frac{N^{3/2}}2
 \left((1-1/(4b))^{t-d}+2\vartheta^{N/b}\right)^{1/2},
 \qquad \vartheta=\frac32\,2^{-3/4}<1.$$ The same bound holds for a fixed ordered list of $N-m$ labels when its conditional distance is averaged over a uniformly chosen starting deck.

Let $d\ge3$, $q=7N/8$, and $T=1024d$. For a uniformly sampled ordered distinct input tuple $C$, independent of $X$, $$\label{shear:eq:random-tuple}
 \mathbb E_C\|\mathcal L(X_T(C)\mid C)-\pi_q\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
 \le\frac{q\sqrt N}{2}
 \left(e^{-16d}+2(N+1)e^{-N/256}\right)^{1/2}=o(1).$$ The corresponding hidden-card energy is $O(N^{-20})$, uniformly over its index.

Fix integers $T\ge1$ and $1\le q\le n$. Let $\mathbf v=(v_1,\ldots,v_T)$ be a random schedule of nonzero directions. Set $Z_0=I$, and suppose that, conditional on $\mathbf v$, the increments of $Z$ are independent uniform switches in directions $v_1,\ldots,v_T$. Let $C=(c_1,\ldots,c_q)$ be a deterministic list of distinct labels, or a random such list independent of the joint process $(\mathbf v,(Z_t)_{t=0}^T)$. For $1\le j\le q$, put $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathcal F_t^{C,j}
 &=\sigma\bigl(C,v_1,\ldots,v_t,\,
       Z_s(c_i):0\le s\le t,\ i<j\bigr), \label{frames:online-field}\\
 \mathcal H^{C,j}
 &=\sigma\bigl(C,\mathbf v,\,
       Z_s(c_i):0\le s\le T,\ i<j\bigr). \label{frames:terminal-field}
\end{aligned}$$ For $0\le t\le T$, define $p_t(x)=\Pr\{Z_t(c_j)=x\mid\mathcal F_t^{C,j}\}$. Then, for every $x\in V$, $$\Pr\{Z_t(c_j)=x\mid\mathcal H^{C,j}\}=p_t(x)
 \quad\text{almost surely}. \label{frames:full-path-prefix}$$ For $t<T$, let $K_t(v)=\Pr\{v_{t+1}=v\mid v_1,\ldots,v_t\}$. Then $$\Pr\{Z_t(c_j)=x,\ v_{t+1}=v\mid\mathcal F_t^{C,j}\}
 =p_t(x)K_t(v). \label{frames:direction-factorization}$$

Fix integers $T\ge d$ and $r\ge1$. For any fixed observed labels and any $d<t\le T$, if $m$ positions remain unobserved, then $$\Pr\{\min(|F_{t-1}\cap J_t|,|F_{t-1}\setminus J_t|)<m/4\}
 \le2e^{-m/8}.$$ For the hidden-card energy of Lemma <a href="#frames:holes" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="frames:holes">38</a>, interpreted in this random process by Corollary <a href="#frames:conditional-interface" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="frames:conditional-interface">39</a>, if $m\ge n/r$ and $\alpha=1/(4r)$, then $$\mathbb E E_T\le(1-\alpha)^{T-d}+2e^{-n/(8r)}.
 \label{frames:crossing-recursion}$$

Let $d\ge4$. Put $b=16$, $m=n/b$, $q=n-m$, and $T=(2+20b)d=322d$. For every fixed ordered list of $q$ distinct input positions, its image under the physical shuffle at time $T$ has distance at most $$\delta_d=\frac{4T}{m}+\frac12n^{3/2}
                (1-1/(4b))^{(T-2d+1)/2}=o(1)
 \label{frames:stopped-delta}$$ from a uniform injection, uniformly over the chosen list.

Define the event on cyclic histories by $$\label{frames:typical-average}
 \mathcal G_{\mathrm{ent}}
 =\bigcap_{m=n/L}^{n}
   \left\{g:\mathbb E_{F:\,|F|=m}Z_T^F(g)\le n^{-8}\right\},$$ where each average is uniform over subsets of labels. Then $\mathbb P(\mathcal G_{\mathrm{ent}})=1-o(1)$.

There is an absolute $C$ such that for every irreducible unitary representation $\rho$ of $G$, of degree $D$, and unit vectors $u,z$, $$\mathbb P\{\mathcal G_{\mathrm{ent}},
       |\langle z,\rho(g_T)u\rangle|\ge D^{-1/8}\}
 \le C D^{-1/(2L)}.
 \label{frames:coefficient-tail}$$ Put $a=\mathbb P(\mathcal G_{\mathrm{ent}})$. When $a>0$, the conditional endpoint law $\eta=\mathcal L(g_T\mid\mathcal G_{\mathrm{ent}})$ is defined and satisfies $$\|\widehat\eta(\rho)\|_{\rm op}
 \le D^{-1/8}+\frac{C}{a}D^{-1/(2L)}.$$ For all sufficiently large $d$, Lemma <a href="#frames:typical-history" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="frames:typical-history">47</a> gives $a\ge1/2$. In that range the last bound is at most $C'D^{-1/(2L)}$ for the absolute constant $C'=1+2C$.

For the Thorp shuffle on $n=2^d$ labeled cards, $$\max_{\sigma\in S_n}
 \bigl\|P_d^{512040d}(\sigma,\cdot)-U_n\bigr\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
 =o(1)\qquad(d\longrightarrow\infty),$$ where $U_n$ is the uniform probability measure on $S_n$ and one time step is one physical shuffle. Consequently, at total-variation threshold $1/4$, $$d\le t_{\mathrm{mix}}(d)\le512040d$$ for all sufficiently large integers $d$.

Let $C=(a_1,\ldots,a_k)$ be a uniformly chosen ordered list of distinct initial cards, independent of $X_T\sim\mu$, and let $\pi_k$ be uniform on ordered $k$-tuples of distinct positions. Then $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb E_C\bigl\|\mathcal L(X_T(C)\mid C)-\pi_k\bigr\|_{\rm TV}
 &\le\varepsilon_n,\notag\\
 \varepsilon_n
 &=\frac{k}{2}\sqrt{n\bigl(e^{-25d}+T\delta_n\bigr)}=o(1).
 \label{frames:aux:marginal-error}
\end{aligned}$$

If $\rho$ is an irreducible unitary representation of $G=S_n$ of dimension $D$, then the measure in <a href="#frames:aux:cosets" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="frames:aux:cosets">[frames:aux:cosets]</a> satisfies $$\label{frames:aux:operator-bound}
 \|\widehat\nu(\rho)\|_{\mathrm{op}}
 \le C_rD^{-1/2+11/r},\qquad C_r=\sqrt{2rK_0}.$$

Let $b=2^h$ and let $Y$ apply each of the $h$ coordinate layers once, in any order, using independent fair switches. For every irreducible representation $\rho_\alpha$ of $S_b$, put $T_\alpha=\mathbb E\rho_\alpha(Y)$. Uniformly for all sufficiently large $b$, $$\frac{\operatorname{tr}(T_\alpha^*T_\alpha)}{D_\alpha}
 =\frac{\operatorname{tr}(T_\alpha T_\alpha^*)}{D_\alpha}
 \le2D_\alpha^{-1/100}.$$

Let $s=2^\ell$ and let $G_s$ be a sweep using each of the $\ell$ coordinate directions once, in any order, with independent fair switches. There are absolute $a>0,C_1<\infty$ such that, for every sufficiently large $s$ and every complex irreducible representation $\rho_\alpha$ of $S_s$, $$q_\alpha:=D_\alpha^{-1}
  \left\|\mathbb E\rho_\alpha(G_s)\right\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2
 \le C_1D_\alpha^{-a}.$$ The Hilbert–Schmidt norm uses the ordinary, unnormalized trace.

Partition a set of size $2s$ into two ordered sets $J_1,J_2$, each of size $s$. Let $A_0$ be a random permutation. Suppose the ordered image of $J_i$ under $A_0$ has total-variation distance $\epsilon_i$ from a uniform injection. Let $\epsilon_{\mathrm{part}}$ be the distance of the ordered output partition $(A_0J_1,A_0J_2)$ from its uniform law. There is a permutation law $A$ whose two ordered image marginals are exactly uniform and such that $$\label{char:repaircost}
 \|\mathcal L(A)-\mathcal L(A_0)\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
 \le\epsilon_1+\epsilon_2+3\epsilon_{\mathrm{part}}.$$ Conditional on its output partition, each of the two bijections is uniform. Their joint law may be dependent.
